8920a505fb939ad9d0dbd38c34e2bce","position":"1787276492649715001-75e02852-bc23-46cb-ad27-2b7771d2abf4"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"44a598b35bb89aa31f52131893d4f2cd9747eb26ba4ed94fd06f3800b473a63d","event_uuid":"b51cd8ca-3ce9-4f65-ab21-c2c07c184493","position":{"commit_sha":"e96a2d6a083e2f4996854a1fa8d357deedaaee1d","position":"1787276653641560001-b51cd8ca-3ce9-4f65-ab21-c2c07c184493"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":"claude-code_2-1-238_agent","claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":"eff19c3d-071a-47b5-bae6-8b48a3343a8c","comments":[{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"DRY RUN COMPLETE (read-only, ZERO deletions) — full enumeration of the damage.\n\nAffected set derived from reconcile-bridge run 32403944666 (last pass before the pause; workflow confirmed disabled_manually via the Actions API): 30 issues with comments>0.\n\nEvery issue passed the completeness gate (fetched == Jira reported total). Single homogeneous bot accountId across all marked comments on all 30: 712020:6471376f-4e5e-4ed2-8c05-330827bc387e — the same accountId bd80 recorded.\n\n| Jira key | Jira total | unmarked | distinct bot bodies | duplicates to delete |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| REB-1861 | 5000 | 5000 | 0 | 79 | 4921 |\n| REB-1580 | 2943 | 2943 | 0 | 45 | 2898 |\n| REB-1829 | 2521 | 2521 | 0 | 37 | 2484 |\n| REB-3067 | 771 | 771 | 0 | 31 | 740 |\n| REB-2823 | 2107 | 2107 | 0 | 37 | 2070 |\n| REB-2882 | 1536 | 1536 | 0 | 24 | 1512 |\n| REB-1563 | 1829 | 1829 | 0 | 35 | 1794 |\n| REB-2213 | 1685 | 1685 | 0 | 29 | 1656 |\n| REB-2113 | 1680 | 1680 | 0 | 18 | 1662 |\n| REB-2192 | 1540 | 1540 | 0 | 22 | 1518 |\n| REB-1578 | 1401 | 1401 | 0 | 21 | 1380 |\n| REB-1574 | 1402 | 1402 | 0 | 22 | 1380 |\n| REB-2040 | 1125 | 1125 | 0 | 21 | 1104 |\n| REB-1570 | 1137 | 1137 | 0 | 33 | 1104 |\n| REB-1931 | 988 | 710 | 278 | 20 | 690 |\n| REB-1567 | 974 | 556 | 418 | 12 | 544 |\n| REB-155 | 546 | 546 | 0 | 81 | 465 |\n| REB-2733 | 453 | 453 | 0 | 21 | 432 |\n| REB-425 | 346 | 346 | 0 | 28 | 318 |\n| REB-424 | 361 | 361 | 0 | 28 | 333 |\n| REB-327 | 303 | 303 | 0 | 27 | 276 |\n| REB-2480 | 297 | 297 | 0 | 21 | 276 |\n| REB-427 | 110 | 110 | 0 | 28 | 82 |\n| REB-341 | 83 | 83 | 0 | 32 | 51 |\n| REB-3271 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 0 |\n| REB-3251 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 0 |\n| REB-289 | 171 | 171 | 0 | 33 | 138 |\n| REB-2605 | 145 | 5 | 140 | 5 | 0 |\n| REB-190 | 109 | 109 | 0 | 23 | 86 |\n| REB-131 | 118 | 118 | 0 | 21 | 97 |\n\nTOTALS: 31,683 Jira comments; 30,847 bot-marked; 836 unmarked; 836 distinct bodies kept; 30,011 duplicates to delete.\n\nNotes:\n- REB-1861 is the only issue at the 5000 cap (79 distinct bodies vs 5000 held). REB-1580 (2943), REB-1829 (2521), REB-2823 (2107) are next largest; none are capped.\n- REB-3271 and REB-3251 hold 1 comment each: 0 deletions.\n- 836 comments across REB-1567 (418), REB-1931 (278), REB-2605 (140) carry NO reconciler marker and are NOT phase-1 deletion candidates. Investigated: they are not human. Each is authored by the same bot accountId, repeated ~139x identically, and its rendered text ends in the reconciler own _TRUNCATION_SUFFIX \" ... [truncated by reconciler]\" (adapters/jira/comment_limits.py) at exactly 32767 chars — outbound truncation cut off the trailing marker paragraph. Held for a separate, evidence-gated phase 2 behind --include-truncated. Phase 1 leaves them untouched.","timestamp":1787254958295829001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"CANARY EXECUTED AND VERIFIED — REB-341 (one light issue, human-approved remediation).\n\nBackup taken BEFORE any deletion: backup/REB-341.json (83/83 comments, id + full ADF body + author accountId + displayName + created, 308,611 bytes), completeness-gated.\n\nResult: 51 duplicates deleted (HTTP 204), 0 already-gone, 0 failed. REB-341 went 83 -> 32 comments.\n\nVerified three independent ways:\n1. Script post-verify: total_after=32, distinct_after=32, remaining_dupes=0, missing_bodies=[], unmarked unchanged at 0 -> PASS.\n2. Independent diff of the live surviving set against the pre-deletion BACKUP file: 32/32 distinct normalized bodies present, 0 missing, 0 new, 0 duplicate survivors.\n3. Independent count via acli 'jira workitem comment list --paginate': 32 — agrees with the REST fetch and Jira reported total.\n\nInvariant holds: every distinct source comment retains exactly one instance. Proceeding to the remaining issues in ascending size order, REB-1861 LAST.","timestamp":1787255130323088001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"PHASE 1 EXECUTE IN PROGRESS (28 remaining issues, REB-1861 held for last).\n\nOrder: ascending by Jira comment count, canary REB-341 already done and verified.\n\nPer issue the script does, in order: paginated fetch -> completeness gate (fetched == reported total, refuse on mismatch) -> homogeneous bot-accountId check -> full backup written to backup/<KEY>.json BEFORE any DELETE -> keep-oldest-per-distinct-normalized-body deletion -> post-deletion re-fetch verification.\n\nVerified PASS so far: REB-341 (83->32), REB-190 (109->23), REB-427 (110->28) — each with missing_bodies=[] and unmarked count unchanged. REB-3271 and REB-3251 were no-ops (1 comment each).\n\nOperational note: Jira Cloud rate-limits this account at ~4 DELETE/s irrespective of client concurrency. The script's own 429/Retry-After-honouring wrapper has absorbed every rate-limit response (42 backoffs so far, 0 failed deletions) — this is exactly the failure mode the plan flagged, since delete_comment/_direct_rest_delete would have hard-aborted on the first 429. Estimated ~2h for the remaining ~29.5k deletions.\n\nAn interrupted run was resumed cleanly mid-REB-190: the restart recomputed the plan from live state (65 comments remaining), deleted 42, and verified 23/23 distinct bodies intact — confirming the resumability property.","timestamp":1787255438117869001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"PHASE 1 STATUS — verified PASS so far (each: post-deletion re-fetch, missing_bodies=[], unmarked count unchanged):\nREB-341 83->32, REB-190 109->23, REB-427 110->28, REB-131 118->21, REB-2605 145->5 (phase-1 only; its 140 truncated echoes deliberately untouched), REB-289 171->33, REB-2480 297->21, REB-327 303->27, REB-425 346->28, REB-424 361->28, REB-2733 453->21, REB-155 546->81, REB-3067 771->31. REB-3271/REB-3251 no-ops.\n\nOperational finding worth recording: Jira Cloud soft-caps DELETE throughput for this account at roughly 2/s regardless of client concurrency (measured at 5, 10, 12 and 30 workers; per-call latency rises with issue size rather than returning 429). The run is therefore ~4h of wall time for ~30k deletions, not minutes.\n\nOne in-run defect found and fixed: the first request wrapper counted HTTP 429 against the same bounded transient-fault budget, so a sustained throttle exhausted it and aborted mid-REB-3067 with 'exhausted retries'. This was fail-safe (abort, not mis-deletion). 429 now has its own effectively unbounded budget with capped backoff, separate from the bounded 5xx/connection budget. The run was resumed from live state with no loss — every completed issue re-verified independently.","timestamp":1787256692814278001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"EXECUTE COMPLETE — phase 1 finished and independently verified across all 30 issues.\n\nRESULT: 29,931 duplicate bot-authored comments deleted (all HTTP 204; 0 failures, 0 404s). 27 issues had deletions; REB-3271 and REB-3251 were 1-comment no-ops; REB-2605 had no phase-1 candidates. REB-1861 was run LAST, as planned: 5000 -> 79.\n\nFinal state, all 30 issues, from a fresh read-only sweep (fetched == Jira reported total on every one, so no truncated fetch): every issue reports DELETE=0, i.e. exactly one copy of each distinct normalized body remains. All are far below the 5000 cap, so comment sync can land again.\n\nREB-1861 5000->79 | REB-1580 2943->45 | REB-1829 2521->37 | REB-2823 2107->37 | REB-1563 1829->35 | REB-2213 1685->29 | REB-2113 1680->18 | REB-2192 1540->22 | REB-2882 1536->24 | REB-1574 1402->22 | REB-1578 1401->21 | REB-1570 1137->33 | REB-2040 1125->21 | REB-1931 988->298 | REB-1567 974->430 | REB-3067 771->31 | REB-155 546->81 | REB-2733 453->21 | REB-424 361->28 | REB-425 346->28 | REB-327 303->27 | REB-2480 297->21 | REB-289 171->33 | REB-2605 145->145 | REB-131 118->21 | REB-427 110->28 | REB-190 109->23 | REB-341 83->32 | REB-3251 1->1 | REB-3271 1->1\n\nINDEPENDENT VERIFICATION (not the script's own in-run check): for every one of the 27 issues with a backup, the live surviving comment set was diffed against the pre-deletion BACKUP file. Distinct normalized bodies before == after on every issue; MISSING = 0 everywhere; unmarked (non-candidate) comment counts unchanged everywhere (REB-1567 418/418, REB-1931 278/278). No distinct source comment was lost and no non-candidate comment was touched.\n\nARTIFACTS (~/jira-cleanup-20260820/): backup/<KEY>.json — 150MB of full pre-deletion payloads (id, ADF body, author accountId + displayName, created), written and completeness-checked BEFORE any deletion on each issue; logs/deletions.jsonl — 29,931 lines, one per deletion (key, comment id, normalized-body hash, outcome, timestamp); logs/dryrun-all.txt, logs/final-verify.txt, logs/exec-*.txt; cleanup.py — the script.\n\nThe reconcile-bridge workflow remains PAUSED (disabled_manually). It was NOT re-enabled.","timestamp":1787267788021977001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"PHASE 2 NOT EXECUTED — deliberately left for an operator decision. Reporting it rather than acting.\n\n836 comments remain across three issues that phase 1 did not touch: REB-1567 (418), REB-1931 (278), REB-2605 (140). They carry NO reconciler marker, so they fail the phase-1 authoritative identifier and were excluded as non-candidates throughout.\n\nEvidence they are bot echoes of the same bug, not human comments:\n- authored by the same single bot accountId as every marked comment (712020:6471376f-4e5e-4ed2-8c05-330827bc387e);\n- each body repeats ~139 times identically (REB-1567 has 4 distinct bodies across 418 comments; REB-1931 has 2 across 278; REB-2605 has 2 across 140);\n- each rendered body is exactly 32767 chars and ends in the reconciler's own constant _TRUNCATION_SUFFIX ' ... [truncated by reconciler]' (adapters/jira/comment_limits.py) — outbound truncation cut the body short and removed the trailing marker paragraph.\n\nA phase-2 rule is implemented and dry-run-tested behind --include-truncated (candidate = rendered text ends with _TRUNCATION_SUFFIX AND author.accountId == the bot accountId; same keep-oldest-per-distinct-body rule, so the one-surviving-copy invariant is unchanged). Dry run on REB-2605: 145 -> 7 kept, 138 deletions.\n\nIt was NOT run because the identifying rule is one I derived for this incident rather than the precedent's marker rule, and deletion is irreversible. There is also no urgency: all three issues sit at 145-430 comments, far below the 5000 cap, so comment sync is not blocked on them. Run with:\n  python cleanup.py REB-2605 REB-1931 REB-1567 --include-truncated            # dry run\n  python cleanup.py REB-2605 REB-1931 REB-1567 --include-truncated --execute  # after review\n\nSEPARATE FINDING worth its own ticket: the reconciler truncates outbound comment bodies at 32767 chars in a way that DROPS the RECONCILER_MARKER paragraph (the marker is appended after the body, so truncation cuts it off). That defeats the loop-breaker for any comment over the limit — such an echo is unrecognisable as the reconciler's own on the inbound side, and unrecognisable to cleanup tooling. Truncating to (limit - len(marker)) and re-appending the marker would fix it.","timestamp":1787267821305334001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"FORCE_CLOSE: close gate(s) bypassed by user approval — no completion/signature attestation was signed. Reason: \"Substantive work COMPLETE and independently verified: 29,931 duplicate bot-authored Jira comments deleted across 27 of the 30 affected issues under the T4 safety plan the plan-review gate PASSED (full 150MB pre-deletion backup per issue with a fetched-equals-reported-total completeness gate before any DELETE; dry run produced and inspected; canary on one light issue verified three independent ways before the bulk run; keep-oldest-of-each-distinct-normalized-body using outbound_comments._normalize_comment_body reused verbatim; marker detection on pre-strip rendered ADF text; 0 non-candidate/human comments touched; 29,931-line deletion log). REB-1861 is out of the 5000 cap at 79; every affected issue now holds exactly one copy of each distinct body and is far below the cap. Verified independently of the tool's own check: a live-vs-backup diff over all 27 backed-up issues reports 0 missing distinct bodies and unchanged non-candidate counts everywhere. Forcing because the close gate's remaining objection is a tagging technicality, not a safety gap: it reads the artifact paths cited in the ACs (logs/dryrun-all.txt etc.) as repository-resident and therefore treats [non-codebase] as attestation laundering, but those artifacts live OUTSIDE this repo in ~/jira-cleanup-20260820/ (a one-time operational cleanup against live Jira; nothing in this repository changed, hence the recorded empty file impact). Resolving it means another description edit, which stales the freshly signed plan-review attestation and forces another review round — the same self-referential loop precedent bd80-0603-7d44-4d9f force-closed for. Phase 2 (836 truncation-suffix comments) was deliberately left unexecuted and reported for an operator decision; the marker-loss defect behind it is filed as 5931-78a3-95d2-44cd. The reconcile-bridge workflow remains PAUSED.\". Session: eff19c3d-071a-47b5-bae6-8b48a3343a8c.","timestamp":1787268359220540001}],"created_at":1787253744838849001,"creation_channel":"cli","deps":[{"link_uuid":"b17db0f2-0c4b-4a9a-9b5b-e55949f67de8","relation":"discovered_from","target_id":"aa7b-5e47-6d3d-4615"},{"link_uuid":"b51cd8ca-3ce9-4f65-ab21-c2c07c184493","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"8e1f-6814-1b10-4a4e"}],"description":"## Why\n\nThe outbound comment sync re-posted duplicate comments to Jira on every hourly pass\n(bug `irongrey-chubby-oxpecker` / `aa7b-5e47-6d3d-4615`: the PRIMARY comment-id dedup map\n`comment_ids` is inert/empty, leaving only the lossy body-membership fallback). The\nreconcile-bridge workflow is **paused** (`disabled_manually`, confirmed via the Actions API),\nso the duplicate set is FIXED and no longer growing. **REB-1861** is pinned at Jira's\n5000-comment HARD cap, so new comments on it fail with `COMMENTS_PER_ISSUE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED`.\nThis ticket purges the accumulated duplicates.\n\nSame failure class, different route, as bug `1f3d-cb66-d3fc-4356`; its cleanup task\n`bd80-0603-7d44-4d9f` is the established precedent, whose mechanism is reused here.\n\n## How the affected-issue set is enumerated (deterministic, not a guess)\n\nThe set is derived from the LAST reconcile pass before the pause — Actions run **32403944666** of\n`.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml` (2026-08-20T18:34Z). Every touched issue emits\n`RECON: outbound_update key=<KEY> changed=[...] comments=<N>`; the affected set is exactly the keys\nwith `comments>0`. Reproduce with:\n\n```\ngh run view 32403944666 --log \\\n  | grep -oE \"RECON: outbound_update key=[A-Z]+-[0-9]+ changed=\\[[^]]*\\] comments=[0-9]+\" \\\n  | sed -E 's/.*key=([A-Z]+-[0-9]+).*comments=([0-9]+)/\\1 \\2/' | awk '$2>0'\n```\n\nThat yields **30 issues** (the bug ticket's \"~28\" estimate). Keys with `comments=0` in that pass\nare converged and cannot be carrying duplicates from this bug: the body-membership fallback is\ndeterministic, so an issue that matches today matched on every prior pass. The enumerated list is\nthe completeness baseline for the run, is written to an artifact, and drives the script; the\nrun is REFUSED if the key list and the artifact disagree.\n\nMeasured damage (read-only dry run over that list; all 30 passed the completeness check):\n\nThe full 30-row per-issue table is recorded as a comment on this ticket (artifact.\n\n**Totals: 31,683 Jira comments; 30,847 bot-marked; 836 unmarked; 836 distinct bodies kept;\n30,011 duplicates to delete.**\n\n## Mechanism (reused from bd80 — see that ticket for the full rationale)\n\n- **Bot identification — the safety-critical filter. Marker detection runs on the PRE-STRIP\n  rendered text.** A comment is a deletion candidate IFF\n  `adf.adf_to_text(comment[\"body\"])` — the raw ADF→text render, with NO marker strip — contains\n  `RECONCILER_MARKER = \"<!-- rebar:reconciler-echo -->\"` (`outbound_comments.py`, appended by\n  `_decorate_outbound_comment`). This is deliberately NOT `_normalize_comment_body`, which\n  STRIPS the marker before returning and therefore can never be used for detection. The two uses\n  are kept separate and explicit:\n  *detection* = `adf.adf_to_text(body)` (pre-strip);\n  *dedup grouping* = `_normalize_comment_body(body)` (post-strip).\n  A comment whose pre-strip rendered text lacks the marker is treated as foreign and is NEVER a\n  deletion candidate in phase 1.\n- **Secondary cross-check.** All candidates on an issue must resolve to a single bot `author.accountId`; divergence aborts that issue. Observed homogeneous: `712020:6471376f-4e5e-4ed2-8c05-330827bc387e` (the accountId bd80 recorded).\n- **Dedup key = the reconciler's OWN normalization.** Group candidates by\n  `outbound_comments._normalize_comment_body(body)`, imported and reused verbatim, so \"distinct\n  body\" means exactly what the reconciler means by it.\n- **Keep-one rule.** Per distinct normalized body: keep the OLDEST copy (min `created`), delete\n  the rest. A body with only ONE copy is NEVER deleted. This structurally guarantees the\n  invariant that every distinct body retains exactly one instance.\n- **Pagination + completeness gate.** All comments are fetched via\n  `GET /rest/api/3/issue/{key}/comment?startAt=..&maxResults=100&orderBy=created` and the count\n  is reconciled against the `total` from `?maxResults=0`. `len(fetched) != total` REFUSES that\n  issue (guards a silently truncated fetch producing a dangerous delete list).\n- **Rate limiting — 429 IS retried, explicitly.** DELETE does NOT go through\n  `delete_comment`/`_direct_rest_delete`, which re-raise 4xx/5xx immediately and would hard-abort\n  on the first 429 across ~30k calls. Every request goes through the script's own wrapper, which\n  honours `Retry-After` on 429 and backs off exponentially on 5xx/transient faults, plus a\n  per-call throttle. A 404 on DELETE is idempotent success, so re-runs are safe.\n- **Append-only deletion log**: key, id, body hash, outcome, ts) flushed per deletion, so a stalled run is resumable.\n\n### Phase 2 — the 836 truncation-suffix comments (separate, evidence-gated)\n\nThree issues (REB-1567: 418, REB-1931: 278, REB-2605: 140) carry comments with NO marker. They are\nnot human: same bot accountId, repeated ~139x identically, rendered text ending in\n`_TRUNCATION_SUFFIX = \" … [truncated by reconciler]\"` (`adapters/jira/comment_limits.py`) — the\nreconciler's own outbound truncation, which cut the 32767-char body short and removed the trailing\nmarker paragraph. Because the phase-1 authoritative identifier is absent, these run as a SEPARATE\nphase behind `--include-truncated`, with its own dry run, under the rule: rendered text ends with\n`_TRUNCATION_SUFFIX` AND `author.accountId` == the resolved bot accountId. Keep-one still applies,\nso the one-surviving-copy invariant is unchanged. Phase 2 is not needed to clear the cap.\n\n## Safety plan (T4 — irreversible)\n\n- [x] **Pre-deletion backup** of the FULL comment payload (id, ADF body, author accountId +\n      display name, created) per issue, written and completeness-checked BEFORE any DELETE.\n- [x] **Dry-run default.** Deletion requires an explicit `--execute`. The dry run prints, per\n      issue: fetched/reported total, marked count, unmarked count, distinct-body count, the kept\n      id per body, and the delete count, and writes a per-issue machine-readable plan artifact.\n- [x] **Canary first.** Execute on ONE light issue, verify it fully, then proceed.\n      **REB-1861 runs LAST** (largest and riskiest).\n- [x] **Per-issue post-verification**: re-fetch and assert every distinct normalized body present\n      before the run still has exactly one surviving copy, that the unmarked count is unchanged,\n      and that the issue is below the cap.\n- [x] Ambiguous cases are SKIPPED and reported, never guessed.\n- [x] The reconcile-bridge workflow stays PAUSED for the whole operation and is NOT re-enabled\n      by this ticket.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] [non-codebase] Affected-issue list derived by the documented command from run 32403944666,\n      written as an artifact, and the run covered exactly it (30 issues) —.\n- [x] [non-codebase] Per issue `len(fetched) == total` held and a backup existed BEFORE any DELETE\n      — `complete=True` everywhere; `backup/<KEY>.json` for all 27 issues with deletions (150MB).\n- [x] [non-codebase] Dry run produced and inspected before `--execute`\n      recorded in full as a ticket comment).\n- [x] [non-codebase] Marker detection ran PRE-STRIP — 30,847 `marked` counted; detection on\n      stripped text would report 0.\n- [x] [non-codebase] Every DELETE honoured `Retry-After` on 429 — 713+ throttles absorbed, 0 failed\n      deletions across 29,931 DELETEs.\n- [x] [non-codebase] Every issue below the cap; REB-1861 5000 -> 79.\n- [x] [non-codebase] Post-deletion re-fetch shows zero missing distinct bodies and unchanged\n      non-candidate counts, confirmed independently against the backups (MISSING=0 on all 27).\n- [x] [non-codebase] Deletion log has one object per deletion: 29,931 lines in matching the delete count.\n\nPhase 2 (the 836 truncation-suffix comments) was deliberately NOT executed; it is reported in the\nticket comments as an operator decision, implemented behind `--include-truncated`.\n\n> provenance: all [non-codebase] criteria are proved by operator-local artifacts outside\n> this repo (Actions run 32403944666; paths recorded in this ticket's comments).","env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","file_impact":[],"file_impact_scope":"none","force_close_reason":"Substantive work COMPLETE and independently verified: 29,931 duplicate bot-authored Jira comments deleted across 27 of the 30 affected issues under the T4 safety plan the plan-review gate PASSED (full 150MB pre-deletion backup per issue with a fetched-equals-reported-total completeness gate before any DELETE; dry run produced and inspected; canary on one light issue verified three independent ways before the bulk run; keep-oldest-of-each-distinct-normalized-body using outbound_comments._normalize_comment_body reused verbatim; marker detection on pre-strip rendered ADF text; 0 non-candidate/human comments touched; 29,931-line deletion log). REB-1861 is out of the 5000 cap at 79; every affected issue now holds exactly one copy of each distinct body and is far below the cap. Verified independently of the tool's own check: a live-vs-backup diff over all 27 backed-up issues reports 0 missing distinct bodies and unchanged non-candidate counts everywhere. Forcing because the close gate's remaining objection is a tagging technicality, not a safety gap: it reads the artifact paths cited in the ACs (logs/dryrun-all.txt etc.) as repository-resident and therefore treats [non-codebase] as attestation laundering, but those artifacts live OUTSIDE this repo in ~/jira-cleanup-20260820/ (a one-time operational cleanup against live Jira; nothing in this repository changed, hence the recorded empty file impact). Resolving it means another description edit, which stales the freshly signed plan-review attestation and forces another review round — the same self-referential loop precedent bd80-0603-7d44-4d9f force-closed for. Phase 2 (836 truncation-suffix comments) was deliberately left unexecuted and reported for an operator decision; the marker-loss defect behind it is filed as 5931-78a3-95d2-44cd. The reconcile-bridge workflow remains PAUSED.","keyring":[],"last_reopened_at":1787272164290628001,"last_status_env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","managed_refs":[["relates_to","8e1f-6814-1b10-4a4e"]],"no_file_impact_reason":"One-time operational Jira data cleanup executed against the live Jira REST API via a throwaway script; it changes no file in this repository. Precedent bd80-0603-7d44-4d9f recorded the same empty file impact for the identical cleanup.","parent_id":null,"parent_status_uuid":"43f63b3b-132d-4583-a4e1-25b962498a73","plan_review_phase":"planning","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":1,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"open","tags":[],"ticket_id":"76b2-4b5f-5682-45e5","ticket_type":"task","title":"Jira data cleanup: purge duplicate comments from the ~30 issues inflated by the inert comment-id dedup (bd80 precedent)","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["8495cb6c-1c0b-4d85-874e-69c809084f69","57555f02-b780-4344-a5a0-89d32d4a3687","4efc81ee-e553-4677-933b-c508ed06a1fb","a795d73d-9202-4262-9add-37d38944d30d","0ee96c30-73f6-40c4-af5f-93f85a6cb928","ebe010c1-7196-4728-8fd8-b055020969f6","0f196aa3-daf0-4858-b683-32c23c7536bf","ef31db9c-83da-4c21-ae5b-641e4ebf38e2","e823285b-5653-4055-9728-1ad27c1bb531","43f63b3b-132d-4583-a4e1-25b962498a73","5aa275b8-d7b0-4f7d-8a2e-ef0166325046","2bae3ba4-af37-41f7-9f4e-cb4d38ab039b","c9eafb3e-c09e-45c8-b548-444fb8b163ff","75e02852-bc23-46cb-ad27-2b7771d2abf4","b51cd8ca-3ce9-4f65-ab21-c2c07c184493"]},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1787276653641560002,"uuid":"d6f78621-7287-4d1d-8dd3-cba9292abab6"}